def manhattan_distance(p1, p2):
    return abs(p1[0] - p2[0]) + abs(p1[1] - p2[1])
```

The Manhattan distance is the sum of the absolute differences of the coordinates, so the implementation applies abs() to both the x- and y-differences and adds them. The tuples may contain integers or floats of any sign because abs() returns the magnitude of any number. It never references the specific points from the three examples and works for any valid input pair of the form (x, y).

An untested input that I tried is ((-2, 5.5), (7, -1.5)), which gives 9 + 7 = 16, and the function returns 16.

