def bounding_box(points):
    min_x, min_y = max_x, max_y = points[0]
    for x, y in points:
        if x < min_x:
            min_x = x
        elif x > max_x:
            max_x = x
        if y < min_y:
            min_y = y
        elif y > max_y:
            max_y = y
    return (min_x, min_y, max_x, max_y)
```

The function sets its initial search limits to the coordinates of the first point and then revisits all the points to expand the limits as needed: larger x goes into max_x, smaller x into min_x, and the same for y. Because each comparison takes only one new point and not the whole set, a point added after the first can still adjust the limits. For a single-point list the two limits on each axis are the same, producing a box of zero width and height, which is what the second test expects. All ranges or lists of mixed signs are allowed, since only comparisons are used.
